  "section_notes": "S2 was weakened: it sets a 5% threshold that triggers only retesting and corrective action, with no sales stop, which protects Northwind's Hearth 10 (6.2%). S5 was also weakened: the 8-per-100k incident level triggers only regulator review, with recall left to regulator discretion and no numeric mandatory trigger, which protects Halcyon's Ember 8 (9.9). S3 (six-year retrofit) favours Northwind's low shut-off coverage, while S4 (36 months) sits inside the acceptable range and favours Halcyon. S1 is strong and applies to both companies' unreported incidents. Per-model results go to customers, not the public, so the red-line solution was reached."
